[49.754746, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[3;2H  \u001b[90m├──────────────┼─────────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────────┤\r\n\u001b[3C│\u001b[m sso-bridge   \u001b[90m│\u001b[m v2.9.0  \u001b[90m│\u001b[m 284,599  \u001b[90m│\u001b[m 5,695    \u001b[90m│\u001b[m 2.0          \u001b[90m│\u001b[m                                          \r\n   \u001b[90m├──────────────┼─────────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────────┤\u001b[39C\u001b[m   \r\n   \u001b[90m│\u001b[m web-login    \u001b[90m│\u001b[m v7.3.1  \u001b[90m│\u001b[m 710,713  \u001b[90m│\u001b[m 14,330   \u001b[90m│\u001b[m 2.0          \u001b[90m│\u001b[m                                          \r\n   \u001b[90m└──────────────┴─────────┴──────────┴──────────┴──────────────┘\r\n\u001b[108C\u001b[m\u001b[1K\r\n   \u001b[1mWhy it's expensive\u001b[m (from the SPL; I have no "]
[49.754763, "o", "per-stage timings)\r\n\u001b[108C\u001b[1K\r\n   1. \u001b[1;37m index=* \u001b[39m with no time bound.\u001b[m It scans every index over the default time range, then filters\r\n\u001b[6C\u001b[37m sourcetype \u001b[m afterwards with \u001b[37m where \u001b[m. Putting \u001b[37m index=… sourcetype=portal:auth \u001b[m in the base search\r\n\u001b[5C\u001b[1K would let Splunk skip irrelevant data.\r\n   2. \u001b[1mTwo full scans.\u001b[m The main search and the \u001b[37m join \u001b[m subsearch both read the same data. The second is just\r\n\u001b[5C\u001b[1K the failure subset of the first.               \u001b[56C┃\r\r\n   3. \u001b[1;37m join \u001b[39m is unnecessary.\u001b[m It is a non-streaming command with subsearch limits. One pass with \u001b[37m stats \u001b[6C\u001b[m┃\r\r\n\u001b[5C\u001b[1K \u001b[37mcount() AS attempts, count(eval(result=\"failure\")) AS failures BY service, version \u001b[m gives the same\u001b[5C┃\r\r\n      output.                                                       \u001b[41C┃\r\r\n   4. \u001b[1mDead \u001b[37m eval is_failure \u001b[39m.\u001b[m It is computed for every event but never used.\u001b[33C┃\r\r\n\u001b[108C\u001b[1K ┃\r\r\n   The scan reached 1.42M events"]
[49.754785, "o", ". The rare 30.8% failure rate for \u001b[37m v4.12.0 \u001b[m stands out against about 2%\r\n   elsewhere, so the result set is tiny and nearly all the cost is in the scan.\r\n\u001b[108C\u001b[1K\r\n   \u001b[1mNext step:\u001b[m ask Splunk AI Assistant for the optimized query (\u001b[37m saia_generate_spl \u001b[m, \u001b[37m spl_only \u001b[m true) and run\r\n   it the same way. I'll then compare runtime, events, results and rows against this baseline. Want me to\r\n   proceed?                                                         \r\u001b[6B \u001b[1m←\u001b[m open sidebar · Interactive · Manual Approval · \u001b[1m/\u001b[m commands · \u001b[1m?\u001b[m help · \u001b[1mtab\u001b[m next tab\u001b[30;3H\u001b[?25h"]
